  (B) RESTATEMENT TEST -- executed, not asserted.  Try to derive the candidate from v^4 = G M_b a_0.
      v^4 = G M_b a_0 is the ISOLATED deep-MOND limit.  Written as a discrepancy it says
          M_dyn/M_b = nu(y_own) -> sqrt(a_0/g_bar,own)  in the deep limit,
      i.e.  d log(M_dyn/M_b)/d log g_bar,own = -1/2 EXACTLY, and it contains NO external field.
      The candidate says that slope is ~0 and the ratio is set by g_ext instead.  The derivation cannot
      close: the BTFR has no g_ext in it and the theorem has no M_b in it.  ==> NOT a restatement of the
      BTFR/RAR/deep-MOND limit.  is_restatement = FALSE on that axis.
      numerically: isolated-RAR slope over the UDGs' own y range = -0.4853 (deep limit -0.500)
  [PASS] restatement test executed: BTFR slope in own g_bar is -1/2, theorem's is ~0 -- they differ   (BTFR gives -0.485, theorem gives ~0.00)
      LITERATURE, against interest: the total-phantom-mass-in-an-external-field result IS published --
      Milgrom 1986 (ApJ 302, 617) and Milgrom 2009 (arXiv:0906.4817) beta(eta); the anisotropic operator
      nu_e(delta_ij + L_e n_i n_j) is Milgrom 1986/2010.  CRITERION (4) FAILS FOR THE THEOREM ITSELF.
      Only the Route A closed form and the population SHAPE test are unstated -- and (see below) the
      shape test is what this script kills.

  (C) COMA UDGs (Freundlich+2022, on disk).  log g_bar and log g_obs at R_e are tabulated INDEPENDENTLY
      and the distance is the cluster's, common to every object.
      11 UDGs read

      --- amplitude, both footings, both external-field conventions ---
      canonical y_e from dynamical: median y_e  0.608  predicted log ratio +0.216  observed +1.390  ==> residual +1.085 dex (scatter 0.216, 16.7 sigma)
      canonical y_e from newtonian: median y_e  0.233  predicted log ratio +0.357  observed +1.390  ==> residual +0.895 dex (scatter 0.236, 12.6 sigma)
      alt       y_e from dynamical: median y_e  0.504  predicted log ratio +0.241  observed +1.390  ==> residual +1.057 dex (scatter 0.217, 16.2 sigma)
      alt       y_e from newtonian: median y_e  0.170  predicted log ratio +0.408  observed +1.390  ==> residual +0.845 dex (scatter 0.239, 11.7 sigma)
      MOST FAVOURABLE convention for the framework: ('alt', 'newtonian') -> +0.845 dex
      anisotropy floor (the local ratio runs from nu_e(1+L_e) to nu_e; the sphere average sits between):
        at median y_e = 0.233:  parallel +0.203 dex, perpendicular +0.417 dex, sphere-average +0.357 dex  -> modelling floor 0.214 dex
  [FAIL] Coma UDG amplitude matches the theorem within 0.3 dex (any convention, any footing)   (best is ('alt', 'newtonian') at +0.845 dex)

      --- SHAPE TEST 1: does the discrepancy ignore the UDG's OWN baryons? ---
      THE CONFOUND (bug pattern 5): the ordinate log(g_obs/g_bar) contains the abscissa log(g_bar).
      Var(log g_bar) measured = 0.02013;  mean measurement variance = 0.01029
      ==> the TRUE spread in log g_bar is only 0.099 dex against a 0.101 dex
          measurement error.  The error is 1.02x the signal.
      unconfounded predictions:  EFE theorem -0.0815   isolated RAR -0.4784
      OBSERVED slope of log(g_obs/g_bar) on log(g_bar): -0.187 +- 0.579 (bootstrap)
      Monte Carlo under EFE-law truth    : -0.557 +- 0.571   -> observed is +0.65 sigma
      Monte Carlo under isolated-RAR truth: -0.760 +- 0.537   -> observed is +1.07 sigma
      SEPARATION between the two hypotheses AFTER the confound: 0.37 sigma
      -> the shared-g_bar confound drags BOTH predictions to the same place; the propose stage compared
         the observed -0.187 against the UNCONFOUNDED -0.021 and -0.478 and called it 'closer to the EFE
         law'.  Against the confounded distributions it is not.
  [FAIL] the Coma own-g_bar shape test separates the two hypotheses at >= 2 sigma   (0.37 sigma)

      --- SHAPE TEST 1b: the UNCONFOUNDED form -- regress log g_obs on log g_bar (no shared variable) ---
      predicted slope   EFE theorem 0.918   isolated RAR 0.522   (both x the regression-dilution factor 0.489 = 0.449 / 0.255)
      OBSERVED: +0.813 +- 0.591
      -> +0.62 sigma from the EFE law, +0.94 sigma from the isolated RAR
  [FAIL] unconfounded shape test prefers the EFE law over the isolated RAR by >= 2 sigma   (|z_RAR|-|z_EFE| = +0.33)

      --- SHAPE TEST 2: the ENVIRONMENT axis (NEW here; shares nothing with g_obs or g_bar) ---
      theorem predicts d log(M_dyn/M_b)/d log g_ext = -0.3896
      isolated RAR (no environment at all) predicts  +0.0000
      OBSERVED: +0.227 +- 0.406   -> +1.52 sigma from the theorem, +0.56 sigma from no-environment
  [FAIL] the environment axis detects the predicted g_ext dependence at >= 2 sigma   (0.56 sigma from zero, and the theorem's own prediction is only -0.390)
      NOTE against interest: the theorem's own environment slope is only -0.3 over a sample spanning
      0.72 dex in g_ext, i.e. a 0.282 dex swing against a
      0.203 dex scatter.  The axis is clean but the lever is small.

